    "result_text": "Done. The full suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, the acceptance test `coreRejectsOverDailyLimit` passing).\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the daily owner-creation limit (reject with **429** once 100+ owners share today's `registrationDate`), following the existing pattern already used for the per-city cap and duplicate checks:\n\n1. **Repository** \u2014 added `countByRegistrationDate(LocalDate)` to the `OwnerRepository` interface and to all three implementations so every profile stays consistent:\n   - `SpringDataOwnerRepository` (a `@Query` count \u2014 this is the profile the tests use)\n   - `JpaOwnerRepositoryImpl` (JPQL count)\n   - `JdbcOwnerRepositoryImpl` (SQL count on `registration_date`)\n\n2. **Exception** \u2014 new `DailyOwnerLimitExceededException`, mirroring `CityCapacityExceededException`.\n\n3. **Service** \u2014 in `ClinicServiceImpl.createOwner`, after the registration date is resolved (defaulting to `LocalDate.now()`), it counts owners already registered on that date and throws when the count is `>= MAX_OWNERS_PER_DAY` (new constant `= 100`), alongside the existing `MAX_OWNERS_PER_CITY`.\n\n4. **Advice** \u2014 new `@ExceptionHandler` in `ExceptionControllerAdvice` mapping the exception to `HttpStatus.TOO_MANY_REQUESTS`, reusing the shared `detailBuild(...)` `ProblemDetail` construction.\n\nEach piece sits in its existing home (repository counts data, service enforces the rule, advice maps status), reusing the established structures rather than duplicating logic.",
